Key Stats for Predicting Strikeouts
Recent Strikeout Rate (K%)

Target batters with a K% > 30% over their last 5–10 games (use FanGraphs or Baseball-Reference).

Example: A hitter with 8+ strikeouts in their last 3 games is in a slump.

Swing-and-Miss Metrics

Whiff Rate (SwStr%): A whiff rate > 15% signals frequent swings and misses (via Baseball Savant).

Chase Rate (O-Swing%): A chase rate > 35% indicates poor plate discipline (swinging at pitches outside the zone).

Contact Ability

Contact Rate < 70%: Struggles to make contact, even on pitches in the zone.

Z-Contact% (in-zone contact): Batters with < 80% Z-Contact% are vulnerable to strikeouts on hittable pitches.

Two-Strike Performance

Two-strike K%: Batters with > 40% strikeout rate in two-strike counts often lack clutch adjustments.

Check their swing/take decisions in two-strike counts (e.g., chasing breaking balls low/away).

Pitch-Type Weaknesses

High whiff rates against specific pitches (e.g., sliders or high fastballs).

Example: A batter with a > 40% whiff rate vs. sliders is prone to Ks against pitchers with strong breaking balls.

Contextual Factors
Pitcher Matchup:

Batters facing high-K pitchers (K/9 > 9.0) or pitchers with elite putaway pitches (e.g., Jacob deGrom’s fastball, Spencer Strider’s slider).

Pitchers with a high swinging-strike rate (SwStr%) (> 12%).

Ballpark Factors: Smaller strike zones (umpire-dependent) or hitter-unfriendly parks (e.g., Oracle Park) can amplify K rates.

Lineup Position: Lead-off hitters often see more pitches and work counts, while free-swinging power hitters (e.g., Joey Gallo) are strikeout-prone.

Fatigue/Injury: Batters playing through injuries (e.g., wrist/hand issues) often lose bat speed or plate coverage.

Workflow to Identify Strikeout-Prone Batters
Filter High-K Batters: Use FanGraphs to sort by K% (last 7 days) or strikeouts per game.

Analyze Swing Behavior:

On Baseball Savant, check Whiff% and Chase Rate trends.

Look for rising strikeout rates paired with declining contact rates.

Pitcher Matchup: Cross-reference with the opposing pitcher’s:

Strikeout arsenal (e.g., fastball velocity, slider spin rate).

Pitch usage (does the pitcher rely on the batter’s weak spots?).

DFS/Betting: Target these batters for strikeout props (e.g., "Over 1.5 Ks" on DraftKings) or fade them in DFS lineups.

Streak-Breaking Indicators
A batter’s strikeout slump may end if:

They face a low-K pitcher (K/9 < 7.0) or a pitcher with poor command (high BB%).

Their contact metrics improve (e.g., Z-Contact% jumps 5+% in recent games).

They adjust their approach (e.g., shorter swings, choking up with two strikes).

Tools & Sources
Plate Discipline Stats: FanGraphs Plate Discipline.

Pitch-Level Data: Baseball Savant Statcast Search.

Pitcher Scouting Reports: Brooks Baseball.

Umpire Trends: Umpires with large strike zones (e.g., Angel Hernandez) boost K chances.

Limitations
Small Sample Sizes: A 3-game K streak might be noise. Focus on 5+ games with consistent trends.

Pitcher Form: Even strikeout-prone hitters can exploit struggling pitchers (e.g., fastball velocity dips).

Umpire Variance: A hitter-friendly strike zone can reduce Ks.

Example Workflow
A batter like Joey Gallo (career 38% K rate):

Last 5 games: 12 strikeouts, 40% K rate.

Whiff Rate: 18% (vs. MLB avg 12%).

Matchup: Faces Spencer Strider (14.0 K/9, elite slider).
→ High probability of 2+ strikeouts in the game.

